_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake xilinx-bootbin

The BOOT.BIN is located in the following directory:

	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/boot.bin

The generated BOOT.BIN can be installed to either SD Card or QSPI. You can
refer to the sections "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode" and "4.2.2 QSPI Boot Mode" for
the detailed steps.

Alternatively, you can generate a WIC image with the BOOT.BIN installed by
building wrlinux-image-std instead of xilinx-bootbin:

	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

Then, you can refer to the section "5. WIC Notes" to flash this WIC image
to SD card.

7.1.4 SDT Flow
--------------
7.1.4.1 Introduction
--------------------
The PMU and FSBL firmwares shown in the Figure 1 "BOOT.BIN structure" are
all embeddedsw components. The legacy methodology of building these
embeddedsw components takes .xsa as a hand-off file from hardware persona
and uses mdd, mld and mss files for different software configurations.
This makes the legacy approach require AMD proprietary tools such as the
software command-line tool (XSCT) and hardware software interface (HSI).
Without these proprietary tools, the embeddedsw components cannot be
compiled with the legacy approach.

The system device tree (SDT) flow aims to avoid such dependencies and
embrace the open-source industry standards without compromising the
performance and the functionality. It uses the system device tree, CMAKE
and a Python-based open source tool named Lopper to organize the required
Build System.

For a more detailed description about porting embeddedsw to SDT flow, refer to
	https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/2743468485/Porting+embeddedsw+components+to+system+device+tree+SDT+based+flow

This process can be roughly illustrated with the following diagram:

                 +------------------+
               +-|Full Bitstream XSA|------------+
     +------+  | +------------------+            |
     |Vivado|--+                                 +--+
     +------+  | +-----------------------------+ |  |
               +-|Dynamic Function eXchange XSA|-+  |
                 +-----------------------------+    |
                        +------------------+        |
                        |system device tree|<-------+ SDTGen
                        +------------------+
                            | gen-machineconf
                  +------------------------+      +----------+
                  |Yocto machine conf files|--+   |BOOT.BIN  |
                  +------------------------+  |   |system.dtb|
    +--------------------------------------+  |-->|boot.scr  |--+
    |meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-standalone    |  |   +----------+  |   +---------+
    |meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-standalone-sdt|  |                 |-->|WIC image|
    |meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-core          |--+                 |   +---------+
    |meta-xilinx/meta-microblaze           |                    |
    +--------------------------------------+  +--------------+  |
                                   +-------+  |Image         |  |
                                   |WRLinux|->|rootfs.tar.bz2|--+
                                   +-------+  +--------------+

						Figure 4 SDT Flow

Topics that apply to this design flow include:
	- Section 7.1.4.2 Vivado design
	- Section 7.1.4.3 SDTGen
	- Section 7.1.4.4 Gen-machineconf
	- Section 7.1.4.5 SDT Flow in WRLinux

7.1.4.2 Vivado Design
---------------------
The SDT flow begins with the XSA files generated by Vivado. According to
how the programming logic (PL) portion of a XSA file is structured and
reconfigured, the XSA files can be divided into the following types:
Full Bitstream design and Dynamic Function eXchange (DFX) design. DFX is
not currently supported.

7.1.4.3 SDTGen
--------------
SDTGen is a tool that implements a Tcl interface to generate system device
trees for AMD FPGAs using an XSA file as input. It outputs not only
hardware configuration information in the form of System Device Trees, but
also binary files containing "firmwares" (initial bootloaders, hardware
configuration files) for the device.

Ensure that Vivado or Vitis is installed on your build-host before executing
the following commands to convert an XSA file into a system device tree:

	(build-host)$ source <installed_vivado_path>/settings64.sh
	(build-host)$ sdtgen -xsa <your_xsa_file> -dir <sdt_outdir> -board_dts zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.1 -debug enable

For a more detailed description of SDTGen, refer to
	https://github.com/Xilinx/system-device-tree-xlnx/blob/master/README.md

7.1.4.4 gen-machineconf
-----------------------
The gen-machineconf is used to extract the necessary information from the
system device tree, create Yocto machine configurations and isolate the
Linux device tree from the broader system device tree.

For a more detailed description of gen-machineconf, refer to
	https://github.com/Xilinx/gen-machine-conf/blob/xlnx_rel_v2025.2/README.md

The gen-machineconf decides how to handle the bitstream file and the PL
dtsi file when generating BOOT.BIN.

	- Without "-g" option, the bitstream file is integrated into BOOT.BIN
	  and loaded in the early boot stage, the PL dtsi file will be merged
	  into system.dtb. After Linux boots up successfully, the PL works. We
	  consider it as a "without SDT pl overlay" mode.

	- With "-g full" option, the bitstream file and the PL dtsi file will NOT
	  be handled. You need to follow the instructions in the section
	  "7.2 Device Tree Overlay" to pack the PL pdi and the PL dtbo into
	  rootfs, and execute the commands in the section "7.3 PL Programming"
	  to load the PL firmware with dtbo using the fpgautil command line
	  utility or configfs. We consider it as a "with SDT pl overlay" mode.

Ensure that the directory <std_outdir> is generated successfully in the
section "7.1.4.3 SDTGen" before executing the following commands:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
BB_NO_NETWORK = '0'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:openembedded-layer += 'kconfig-frontends'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:meta-python += 'python3-humanfriendly'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:virtualization-layer += 'lopper'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'python3-anytree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-lops'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'esw-conf'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
_EOF
	(build-host)$ export PATH=$PATH:<PROJECT_DIR>/layers/meta-xilinx/gen-machine-conf/

For the "without SDT pl overlay" mode, run the following commands:
	(build-host)$ gen-machineconf parse-sdt --hw-description <std_outdir> -c conf/ --machine-name <workflow_machine_name> --add-config CONFIG_SUBSYSTEM_MACHINE_NAME=\"zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.1\"

For the "with SDT pl overlay" mode, run the following commands:
	(build-host)$ gen-machineconf parse-sdt --hw-description <std_outdir> -c conf/ --machine-name <workflow_machine_name> --add-config CONFIG_SUBSYSTEM_MACHINE_NAME=\"zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.1\" -g full

After executing either of the two commands above, the following folders
and files will be generated under <PROJECT_DIR>/build:
	conf/machine/<workflow_machine_name>.conf
	conf/machine/include/<workflow_machine_name>/
	conf/dts/<workflow_machine_name>/
	hw-description/<workflow_machine_name>/
	output/<workflow_machine_name>/

You may observe the output below telling you to set "MACHINE" during
gen-machineconf:

	# Use the newly generated MACHINE
	MACHINE = "<workflow_machine_name>"

This output message can be ignored because <workflow_machine_name> will
be assigned to "WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME" in conf/local.conf described in
the section "7.1.4.5 SDT Flow in WRLinux". The Yocto machine conf files
generated by gen-machineconf will be treated as an additional machine
configuration. Assigning it to "MACHINE" can cause the WRLinux BSP
layer amd-zynqmp to be excluded, resulting in WRLinux build failure.

The <workflow_machine_name> can be any string. For example, the name
"wrlinux-zcu102" for the "without SDT pl overlay" mode and the name
"wrlinux-zcu102-full" for the "with SDT pl overlay" mode are available
names.

For a more detailed description of gen-machineconf in meta-xilinx, see
	https://github.com/Xilinx/meta-xilinx/blob/rel-v2025.2/meta-xilinx-standalone-sdt/README.sdt.bsp.md

Note: The following names can NOT be used as <workflow_machine_name>
because they have been occupied by the machine conf files in the layer
meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp.
	zynqmp-zcu102-sdt-full

7.1.4.5 SDT Flow in WRLinux
---------------------------
After running the steps in the section "7.1.4.4 gen-machineconf", run the
following steps to build a WRLinux image:

	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME = "<workflow_machine_name>"
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " xilinx-bootloader"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-uenv'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fsbl'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'pmufw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bitstream'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'trusted-firmware-a'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'pmu-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'fsbl-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone-sdt += 'sdt-artifacts aiefal aienginev2 audio-formatter avbuf axicdma axicdma-example axidma axidma-example axiethernet axiethernet-example axipcie axipmon axipmon-example axis-switch axivdma axivdma-example bram bram-example can can-example canfd canfd-example canps canps-example cframe cfupmc clk-wiz clk-wiz-example clockps clockps-example common coresightps-dcc csi csi2tx csi2txss csudma csudma-example dcsub ddrcpsu devcfg devcfg-example dfeccf dfeccf-example dfeequ dfeequ-example dfemix dfemix-example dfeofdm dfeofdm-example dfeprach dfeprach-example dfxasm dfxasm-example dmaps dmaps-example dp12 dp12rxss dp12txss dp14 dp14rxss dp14txss dp21 dp21rxss dp21txss dpdma dphy dppsu dsi dsi2rx dsirxss dsitxss dual-splitter emaclite emaclite-example emacps emacps-example emc gpio gpio-example gpiops gpiops-example hdcp1x hdcp22-cipher hdcp22-cipher-dp hdcp22-common hdcp22-mmult hdcp22-rng hdcp22-rx hdcp22-rx-dp hdcp22-tx hdcp22-tx-dp hwicap i2srx i2stx i3c i3cpsx i3cpsx-example iic iic-example iicps iicps-example intc intc-example iomodule ipipsu ipipsu-example llfifo llfifo-example mbox mboxpsu-exampmcdma mcdma-example mipicsiss mipi-rx-phy mipi-tx-phy mmidp mutex mutex-example nandps nandpsu nandpsu-example ospipsv ospipsv-example pciepsu pciepsu-example prc prd qspips qspips-example qspipsu qspipsu-example resetps resetps-example rfdc rfdc-example rtcpsu rtcpsu-example scugic scugic-example scutimer scuwdt sd-fec sdiaud sdi-common sdps sdps-example spdif spi spips spips-example sysmon sysmon-example sysmonpsu sysmonpsu-example sysmonpsv sysmonpsv-example tft tmrctr tmrctr-example tmr-inject tmr-manager trafgen trafgen-example trngpsv trngpsx ttcps ttcps-example uartlite uartlite-example uartns550 uartns550-example uartps uartps-example uartpsv uartpsv-example ufspsxc usb usb-example usbps usbpsu usbpsu-example v-axi4s-remap v-csc v-deinterlacer v-demosaic v-demosaict v-gamma-lfrmbuf-rd v-frmbuf-rd-example v-frmbuf-wr v-frmbuf-wr-exa