_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake xilinx-bootbin

The BOOT.BIN is located in the following directory:

	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/boot.bin

The generated BOOT.BIN can be installed to either SD Card or QSPI. You can
refer to the sections "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode" and "4.2.2 QSPI Boot Mode" for
the detailed steps.

Alternatively, you can generate a WIC image with the BOOT.BIN installed by
building wrlinux-image-std instead of xilinx-bootbin:

	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

Then, you can refer to the section "5. WIC Notes" to flash this WIC image
to SD card.

Note: Check if you set WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME correctly in conf/local.conf
when encountering the following build error:
	File "layers/oe-core/bitbake/lib/bb/parse/parse_py/ConfHandler.py", line 97, in include_single_file
	raise ParseError("Could not %s file %s" % (error_out, fn), parentfn, lineno)
	bb.parse.ParseError: ParseError at layers/amd-zynqmp/conf/machine/amd-zynqmp-sdt.inc:5: Could not include required file conf/machine/<workflow_machine_name>.conf

7.1.4.6 Rebuild
---------------
If you want to run gen-machineconf to create a new <workflow_machine_name>
based on an existing project, you need to clean the build files by running
the following commands:

	(build-host)$ rm conf/machine -Rf; rm output -Rf; rm conf/dts -Rf; rm hw-description -Rf
	(build-host)$ sed -i '/WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME/d' conf/local.conf

After re-running the gen-machineconf command, do not forget to reconfigure
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME in conf/local.conf.

7.2 Device Tree Overlay
-----------------------
The Device Tree Overlay(DTO) is used to add the child nodes and the fragments
from the .dtbo file to the base device tree, then triggering drivers to be
probed after PL programming. So the dtbo file for PL needs to be installed
to rootfs along with PL firmwares (including Bitstream or PDI).

The recipe "zynqmp-fw-demo" in the amd-zynqmp BSP layer is designed to
generate the dtbo file in WRLinux by utilizing the bbclass "dfx_user_dts".
For a more detailed description of "dfx_user_dts", please see
	https://github.com/Xilinx/meta-xilinx/blob/rel-v2025.2/docs/README.dfx.user.dts.md

After running the steps in the section "7.1.4 SDT Flow", execute the
following commands:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cp conf/dts/<your_workflow_machine_name>/pl-overlay-full/pl.dtsi <PROJECT_DIR>/layers/amd-zynqmp/recipes-firmware/zynqmp-fw-demo/files
	(build-host)$ cp hw-description/<path_to_bit>/<proj>.bit <PROJECT_DIR>/layers/amd-zynqmp/recipes-firmware/zynqmp-fw-demo/files
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " zynqmp-fw-demo"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:wr-amd-zynqmp += 'zynqmp-fw-demo'
FW_DEMO_FILES:pn-zynqmp-fw-demo = "pl.dtsi <proj>.bit"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

The dtbo and bitstream files can be found at the boards:
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# ls -l /lib/firmware/xilinx/zynqmp-fw-demo/zynqmp-fw-demo.*
	-rw-r--r-- 1 root root    3825 Apr  5  2011 /lib/firmware/xilinx/zynqmp-fw-demo/zynqmp-fw-demo.dtbo
	-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 1423280 Apr  5  2011 /lib/firmware/xilinx/zynqmp-fw-demo/zynqmp-fw-demo.bin

These files can be used to program PL by following the instructions in
the section "7.3 PL Programming".

7.3 PL Programming
------------------
There are 3 methods to program PL from the Linux side:
    - sysfs
    - configfs
    - fpgautil

For a more detailed description of PL programming, please see
	https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/18841847/Solution+ZynqMP+PL+Programming

7.3.1 Sysfs
-----------
Run the following commands to program PL via sysfs:

	root@amd-zynqmp:~# echo 0 > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/flags
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# mkdir -p /lib/firmware
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# cp <path_to_bin/proj.bin> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# echo proj.bin > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/firmware

Note: The sysfs method only supports the PL programming without dtbo.

7.3.2 Configfs
--------------
Run the following commands to program PL via configfs:

	root@amd-zynqmp:~# echo 0 > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/flags
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# mkdir -p /lib/firmware
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# cp <path_to_bin/proj.bin> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# cp <path_to_dtbo/pl.dtbo> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# mkdir /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# cd /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-zynqmp:~# echo pl.dtbo > /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system/path

Steps to remove the drivers that were added as part of DTO

	root@amd-zynqmp:~# rmdir /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system

7.3.3 Fpgautil
--------------
The fpgautil tool provides a user-friendly interface for loading,
reconfiguring and reading FPGA bitstream configurations. Follow
the steps to install this tool on WRLinux:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fpga-manager-script'
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " fpga-manager-script"
_EOF

Run the following commands to program PL via fpgautil:
    root@amd-zynqmp:~# fpgautil -b <path_to_bin/proj.bin> -o <path_to_dtbo/pl.dtbo>


8. Secure Boot
==============

8.1 Introduction
-----------------
This section provides detail description about how to enable secure
boot on ZCU102 rev1.0 board. It includes the implementation principles
of secure boot, implementation example step by step and so on.

8.1.1 Dependency
-----------------
Secure boot feature depends on template feature/secure-boot.

8.1.2 Building Secure Boot
---------------------------

	# ./wrlinux-x/setup.sh --machines amd-zynqmp --templates feature/secure-boot


8.2. Preparations & Prerequisites
---------------------------------
To use secure boot feature, assume that you are:
 - Experienced with WRLinux embedded software development
 - Experienced with Xilinx development tools such as xSDK.
 - Familiar with ARMv8 and ZynMP SoC architecture.
 - Familiar with the basic concepts of security, like openssl, authentication,
   encryption, hardware root trust, RSA, AES, key source, certificate, etc.

Hardware preparations:
 - A host machine installed with Xilinx Design Tool.
 - A host machine installed with aarch64 cross-compile toolchain.
 - Xilinx ZynqMP ZCU102 development sets with Silicon version 4.

8.3. WRLinux ZynqMP Security Introduction & Hardware Root Of Trust Secure Boot
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

WRLinux secure boot on ZynqMP platform includes bootloader(BOOT.BIN) based on the
hardware root of trust secure boot from Xilinx Zynq MPSoC and verifiable fitImage
with RSA2048 signature.

Secure boot encryption and signature overview chart for zynqmp platform as below:

 Encryption & signature                           Verification
 ----------------------                           ------------

 +--------------+               *
 | AES256 key   |               *                +----------------------+
 | RSA4096 pair |               *                | Program key & digest |
 +--------------+   +-> AES key & P-key digest-->| into BBram, eFuse or |
       |            |           *                | boot header		|
       |            |           *                +----------------------+
       |            |           *                       |
       v            |           *                       v
   +---------+      |           *                +-------------+
   | Xilinx  |------+           *                |             |  /-no->lockdown
   | xSDK    |                  *                | CSU verify  | |
   | tools   |------+           *                | and decrypt |<
   +---------+      |           *                | image set   | |
       ^            |           *                |             |  \-yes->u-boot
       |            |           *                +-------------+        |
       |            |           *                       ^               |
  +-------------+   |           *                       |               |
  + Image sets  |   +-> encrypted and signed image------+               v
  + assembling: |               *                                Verify fitImage
  + fslb.elf    |               *                                       |
  + pmufw.elf   |               *                                      yes
  + system.bit  |               *                                       |
  + bl31.elf    |               *                                       |
  + u-boot.elf  |               *                                       v
  +------------+                *    			    Finally boot up WRLinux kernel
       ^
       |
(RSA2048/RSA4096 public key in u-boot,
which is used to verify the signed
fitImage with RSA2048/RSA4096 private key)


8.4 CSU Introduction
--------------------
Hardware CSU processor embedded in ZynqMP SoC to handle security stuffs

ZynqMP holds a triple redundant microblaze processor CSU to handle some security
boottime stuffs like:

  * Interpret the boot header and eFuse registers to configure the system and
    do the validation of the public key and public key revocation.
  * Loading, authentication, decryption(optional) of the FSBL, u-boot, etc.
  * Configure the PL via PCAP & Keys management.
  * Tamper events detecting and response.

CSU module AES-GCM hardware engine supports confidentiality of the boot images
and SHA hardware accelerator implements the SHA-3 algorithm and produces a 384
bits digest, which is used together with the RSA accelerator to provide images
authentication.

Please refer to the chapter 12 in [3] for more secure boot information.


8.5. Using Xilinx Design tools to make BOOT.BIN with AES encryption and RSA signature
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Prerequisites:

  * RSA key pair related knowledges, like using openssl to make RSA key pair.
  * Please carefully read chapter 8, 11, 12 in [3], chapter 8 and 16 in [4],
    chapter 5 in [9] and all content of [5] in References section.
  * Download and install Xilinx SDK v2025.2 or later version via link [7].

After having downloaded and installed Xilinx SDK, then you can start to make
RSA key pair and assemble your own zynqmp BOOT.BIN by bootgen command.

8.5.1. Using bootgen in Xilinx Design tools
-------------------------------------------
Set environment with below command:

   $ source <path_to_XSDK>/settings64.sh

After setting environment, customer is able to generate RSA 4096 key pairs
and BOOT.bin according to the doc [4], [5] and [9].
The following are some simple examples:

 - Generate RSA 4096 key pairs

   $ bootgen -generate_keys auth pem -arch zynqmp -image generate_pem.bif

 - Generate sha3 degist value

   $ bootgen -image generate_hash_ppk.bif -arch zynqmp -w -o test.bin -efuseppkbits sha3.txt

 - Generate BOOT.BIN for key stored in BBRAM

   $ bootgen -image generate_secure_boot_bbram.bif -arch zynqmp -w -o BOOT.BIN

 - Generate BOOT.BIN for key stored in eFUSE

   $ bootgen -image generate_secure_boot_efuse.bif -arch zynqmp -w -o BOOT.BIN

 - Generate BOOT.BIN for key stored in boot header

   $ bootgen -image generate_secure_boot_bh.bif -arch zynqmp -w -o BOOT.BIN

Note:
All the *.bif files and keys are in BSP layer amd-zynqmp/zynqmp_keys.

Warning:
  * BH RSA Option(In chapter 12 of [3]), is only used for integration and test
    support. The corresponding bit set keyword is bh_auth_enable in *.bif used
    by bootgen. The consequently result is that the PPK and SPK-ID
    will not be verified for authentication, so clearly this mode should not be
    used in final product.
    If this option is set in the configuration file and meanwhile the eFUSE is
    programmed to force authentication with "RSA_EN" by Xilinx Tools, this device
    will go into lockdown during boot.


8.5.2 Detail steps about how to generate secure bootloader(BOOT.BIN)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Below example describes how to generate BOOT.BIN and the black key is stored
in boot header.

8.5.2.1 Prepare keys
--------------------

So far, there are all the related files as below to generate BOOT.bin.
	aes*.nky		//AES key to encrypt each partition in BOOT.BIN
	psk0.pem	//The primary secret key, it is from BSP layer amd-zynqmp/zynqmp_keys/hd-root-trusted-rsa4096
	ssk0.pem	//The secondary secret key, it is from BSP layer amd-zynqmp/zynqmp_keys/hd-root-trusted-rsa4096
	helperdata.txt	//PUF(physically unclonable function) Syndrome data
	black_key.txt	//The black key that is a encrypted key
	black_iv.txt	//The black key initialization vector

Please place these keys in the path <your_keys_path>. Then copy the sample
bif file zynqmp_keys/bifs/generate_secure_boot_bh.bif to <your_keys_path>.
And substitute the string "<path_to_place_keys>" in the bif file with <your_keys_path>.
Please do NOT modify paths for the following components in the bif file:
	pmufw.elf
	fsbl.elf
	<your_bitstream_file>
	arm-trusted-firmware.elf
	<your_dtb_file>
	u-boot.elf

8.5.2.2 Build BOOT.BIN with secure boot
---------------------------------------

If the BOOT.BIN with secure boot feature is required, first run the steps described
in the chapter "6.2 Build BOOT.BIN in WRLinux" to modify conf/local.conf, then run
the following commands to add specific variables to configure and build BOOT.BIN:

      $ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
BIF_FILE_NAME = "generate_secure_boot_bh.bif"
BIF_FILE_DIR = "<your_keys_path>"
NKY_FILE_PATH = "<your_keys_path>"
_EOF
	  $bitbake xilinx-bootbin

NOTE: Add the following line to conf/local.conf to enable the detailed decryption output:
	XSCTH_BUILD_DEBUG = "1"

The BOOT.BIN file is created in the deploy dir as follows:
	tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/boot.bin

8.6. Programming BBRAM and eFUSE
--------------------------------
After all the development effort, it is recommended to use BBRAM or eFUSE to
save key for protecting product. About how to burn BBRAM and eFUSE, please
refer to [5].


8.7. A verifiable fitimage
--------------------------

u-boot uses rsa2048/rsa4096 cryptographic algorithms to 'sign' software images.
Images are signed with a private key of the ras2048/rsa4096 key pair that is only
owned by the signer, but can be verified by anyone who has the public key.
In wrlinux, the fitImage is signed with private key during building linux-yocto kernel.
The public key is combined with u-boot.dtb that is in the final image u-boot.elf.
The signature and verification process as follows:


      Signing                                      Verification
      =======                                      ============

 +--------------+                   *
 | 2048/4096 RSA|                   *             +---------------+
 | key pair     |                   *             | Public key in |
 +--------------+       +------> Public key ----->| trusted u-boot|
        |               |           *             +---------------+
        |               |           *                    |
        v               |           *                    v
   +---------+          |           *             +---------------+
   |         |----------+           *             |u-boot verify  |
   | Signer  |                      *             |the fitImage   |
   |         |----------+           *             |signed with the|--> yes/no
   +---------+          |           *             |rsa2048/rsa4096|
        ^               |           *             |private key    |
        |               |           *             +---------------+
        |               |           *                    ^
   +----------+         |           *                    |
   | Software |         +----> Signed fitImage ----------+
   |  image   |                     *
   +----------+                     *

Note:
 - To sign kernel image, Xilinx secure boot feature supports rsa2048 and rsa4096 key type.
   Set KERNEL_RAS_TYPE as the expected value. The default one is rsa2048.


8.8. Enable secure boot for BSP amd-zynqmp
------------------------------------------

8.8.1 Build amd-zynqmp BSP project with secure-boot template
------------------------------------------------------------
Detail steps as below:
	$ mkdir <path_to_project>
	$ cd <path_to_project>
	$ <path_to_wrlinux>/wrlinux-x/setup.sh --machine amd-zynqmp --distro wrlinux --dl-layers --all-layers --accept-eula=yes --templates feature/secure-boot
	$ source ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux
	$ source ./oe-init-build-env
	$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

Note:
When building amd-zynqmp BSP project with secure-boot template, the kernel image type is fitImage that
includes dtb and kernel image. They are all signed by the private key of a RSA-2048/RSA-4096 key that is in
<path_to_project>/layers/amd-zynqmp/zynqmp_keys/fitImage-rsa2048-keys and
<path_to_project>/layers/amd-zynqmp/zynqmp_keys/fitImage-rsa4096-keys

8.8.2 Boot fitImage
-------------------
The fitImage is booted with command "bootm".
There is an example as below, download kernel from tftp server and mount NFS file system.

	# setenv bootargs console=ttyPS0,115200n8 root=/dev/nfs rw rootwait earlycon=cdns,0xFF000000 nfsroot=$serverip:<path_to_nfs> ip=dhcp clk_ignore_unused
	# setenv bootcmd 'tftpboot 0x18000000 $serverip:<path_to_kernel>/fitImage; bootm 0x18000000'
	# setenv ipaddr <board-ip>
	# setenv netmask <board-netmask>
	# setenv gatewayip <board-gatewayip>
	# setenv serverip <server-ip>
	# run bootcmd

There is another example based on wic image, load kernel from sd boot partition and mount file system from /dev/mmcblk0p2.

	# setenv bootfile fitImage
	# setenv loadaddr 0x18000000
	# setenv dtbfile conf-zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0.dtb
	# setenv dtbofile conf-zynqmp-secure-boot.dtbo
	# setenv bootargs console=ttyPS0,115200n8 root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 rw earlycon=cdns,0xFF000000 ip=dhcp clk_ignore_unused
	# setenv bootcmd 'fatload mmc 0:1 $loadaddr $bootfile; bootm ${loadaddr}#${dtbfile}#${dtbofile}'
	# run bootcmd

8.9. Notes
----------

Only encryption and authentication part of CSU are validated, other functions
like tamper event detection and response belongs to CSU bootrom codes, please
contact Xilinx for more information about CSU. Only boot header mode(A black
key stored in boot header) is validated currently.

Risks warning from UG1085, once program efuse wrongly, the board might enter
into a secure lockdown state if SEC_LK is programmed into eFuse. Please refer
to Chapter 8, 11, and 12 in [3] for more security related information.

Not all secure function modules are validated due to hardware resource issue,
like the following:
  SEC_LK, JTAG_DIS, ERR_DIS, BBRAM_DIS, ENC_ONLY, etc.


